  *    workspace (`GET /api/agents` returns `[]` — no registered agent
  *    identities to assign per-process).
  *
+ *    The double-claim path is externally detectable, but not self-healing:
+ *    two successful results for the same task carry different synthetic
+ *    leaseIds with the same Multica issue-id prefix
+ *    (`<issueId>:<uuid>`). A supervising test/observability layer can flag
+ *    that as a duplicate claim, and `cross-instance-claim.test.ts` keeps
+ *    that boundary executable. The two isolated claimers themselves cannot
+ *    detect it at claim time when they share the same token; each re-read
+ *    observes the same `assignee_id` and therefore believes it won.
+ *
  *    **Accepted scope decision (operator-confirmed, post-lock-concurrent-
  *    claim-tests):** P1's spine is a single thin consumer (see
  *    docs/north-star.md, README.md "Shape") — multi-worker coordination is
  *    explicitly P4 ("role-tree / squads / workflows"), not P1. This class
  *    therefore only guarantees claim-safety WITHIN one process (guarantee
  *    #1 above); running more than one MulticaLock-backed process against
- *    the same Multica workspace concurrently, before P4 lands its own
- *    coordination mechanism, WILL silently double-claim. Do not do that.
- *    Revisit this class (or replace it) when P4 actually introduces
- *    multiple workers.
+ *    the same Multica workspace concurrently, before P4 lands a real
+ *    coordination mechanism, can double-claim and must be treated as an
+ *    externally detected duplicate-work incident. Revisit this class (or
+ *    replace it) when P4 actually introduces multiple workers.
  *
  * ## renew() — no server-side equivalent
